<section class="source-section">
  <h2>
    A fairly comprehensive* history** of terminology management in US
    immigration processes
  </h2>

  <h3>Speaking with one voice: By way of introduction, an apologetics</h3>
  <p>
    I once asked my sister, an <i>Actual Linguist</i>, what term I should use to
    refer to the people who do the kind of work I did at the time: translators,
    interpreters, terminologists focusing on managing the conceptual and
    terminological bases for trans-linguistic actions of many types (read:
    localization, internationalization, transcreation, all the -ns).
    Specifically, I asked her if "linguists" was OK as a "basket term". She
    shrugged; one of us suggested "field linguist", and while that might bring
    to mind people like the Summer Institute of Language, it felt close enough
    for me.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the thing about field linguists, that we all know or should have
    reason to know about ourselves, but rarely admit in public:
    <strong>we think we're right</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The closest I've seen to a broad public admission of this was at a lunch
    table of Spanish language interpreters from all over the dialectical map;
    someone said, "Whenever there's a conversation about how to translate a
    specific term, there's always going to be someone who pipes up with
    <i>'Pues en mí país se dice...'</i> (Well, in my country, we say...)"; this
    was greeted with general laughter and agreement.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is our Achilles' heel. Especially when interpreting, though it happens
    in translation as well: in the moment,
    <i>you're the conduit</i>. The job consists of making judgment calls, under
    pressure, under strain, under fatigue, balancing intuition and subconscious
    context with the conscious demands of the moment. And in that moment, that
    fraction of a time, under the best of circumstances, you make decisions. Or
    maybe you rely on your training and practice, especially with the fatigue.
    Or, worst case, 'whatever comes out is what comes out'
    <s>
      <i>que se te salga lo que se te salga</i>
    </s>
    .
  </p>
  <p>
    I am, unabashedly, one of these drama queen field linguists. However, I have
    benefitted from the training and mentorship of many, and several in
    particular, practitioners who helped me understand why training, yes, but
    standardization and broad consensus around terminology management is the
    foundation not just of a skilled professional practice, but of an ethical
    and effective one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>
      In the moment, you are the decider. But in the context of your
      interlocutors, you are just one conduit amongst many. And if we, as a
      whole, are to be what we intend to be--conduits of meaning that neither
      add, nor subtract, nor omit, nor summarize meaning--then we must speak
      with one voice.
    </strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    As another, deeply experienced, colleague once said in a moment of
    frustrated passion as a client insisted they spoke different dialects: "El
    español es uno y <i>solo</i>."***
  </p>

  <h3>
    Everyone wants the documentation to be good; no one wants to maintain it
  </h3>
  <p>
    There came a moment in my career wherein I was presented with a tremendous
    opportunity: the Executive Office for Immigration Review (Immigration Court)
    in Cleveland, Ohio, was hiring, for the first time, a staff interpreter.
    There are, of course, many full-time staff interpreters working in Spanish
    and a few other languages in Immigration Courts all over the United States;
    but we'd never had a full-time presence in Cleveland.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was fortunate enough to be the first hire, quickly followed by wonderful,
    arguably more capable, colleagues. And I had learned from my mentors, one in
    particular, what I needed to do in this context: Build. Make the job bigger
    than me. Take a role and make it an institution. In the context of an
    interpretation department, there are always particularities according to the
    institution, but in many ways it begins and ends with terminology
    management.
  </p>
  <p>
    I had a few glossaries about immigration terms; there was, at the time, an
    official EOIR glossary that was good, but several terms included in it
    referred to case law that was no longer applicable, which made me suspect it
    hadn't been revised in a while.
  </p>
  <p>
    Me being me, I wrote an email to the head of Interpreter Services for the
    EOIR, and asked who was in charge of maintaining the glossary, explaining my
    interest and passion in the topic. The response was, of course, "That's
    amazing! It sounds like you're in charge of it!"
  </p>
  <p>
    I did what I had been trained to do: get all the glossaries I could find,
    open a spreadsheet, and start researching.
  </p>
  <p>
    I knew it was a substantial project. What I did not envision was that it
    would be a year and a half of sustained effort before I had something I
    could consider shareable, and that I would come to deeply understand the
    maxim: "A translation is never finished, merely abandoned."
  </p>
  <p>
    I encountered the historical progression of US Immigration glossaries out of
    chronological order. However, presenting them in that order would bring this
    fully into the genre of memoir, which is not my intent. The following will
    attempt to reconstruct the history of these documents in the order in which
    they were created.
  </p>

  <h2>Early days: Berlitz</h2>
  <p>
    Immigration law in the United States is much younger than many are aware.
    I'll gloss over its earliest origins, unless more, older documents come to
    light. Our account begins, properly, in the era after the Second World War,
    when the conventions surrounding international asylum law were created.
    Countries that became signatories to those agreements necessarily
    incorporated those rules into the processes surrounding their sovereign
    territories. This included things like providing processes for those seeking
    asylum; it contemplated the possibility of managing cross-border flows of
    persons under circumstances of infectious pandemic, and moments of national
    emergency.
  </p>
  <p>
    While there
    <strong>
      is no official language of the United States, nor even an official
      language of law,
    </strong>
    (and, in subsequent decades, we put laws on the books that would prohibit,
    or at least complicate, such a declaration), the
    <i>language of record</i> of the legal system of the United States is
    English.
  </p>
  <p>
    But: Now we are considering contexts which specifically and explicitly
    invole extending that legal system to people who should not be assumed to
    speak English, or not speak it well enough to be linguistically present in
    legal proceedings. The US Governmment did not have the resources on-hand to
    figure out how to handle this. When it came to ensuring that those who did
    <strong>spoke with one voice</strong>, we, as humans, didn't even have a
    codified profession of "interpreter" yet. Consider the fact that the
    profession as such is considered to have been created during the Nuremberg
    Trials.
  </p>
  <p>
    But don't worry, this is the United States. What do we do when we don't have
    the infrastructure to handle something? We contract the private sector to
    handle it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So it was that Berlitz, experts in teaching language and writing tourism
    phrase books of all descriptions, found themselves writing glossaries for
    the first US border agents assigned to police the Mexico-US border.
  </p>
  <p>
    A colleague, when he heard what I was working on, asked me for my address. I
    have no idea where he got these documents; no one in the EOIR that I asked
    knew of them. But true to his word, he sent me a manila envelope, previously
    used as inter-departmental mail from Kent State University, containing four
    documents produced by Berlitz.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first three seemed to have been produced contemporaneously: a
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/Berlitz_List_of_Terms.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      "Term List"
    </a>
    , an
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/Berlitz_English_to_Spanish.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      "English to Spanish Glossary"
    </a>
    , and a
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/Berlitz_Spanish_to_English.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      "Spanish to English Glossary"
    </a>
    . The last document seems to have been produced shortly afterwards, the
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/Berlitz_Supplementary_List.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      "Supplementary List of Immigration Terms"
    </a>
    :
    <i>
      "An additional list of terms that you may encounter in an immigration
      hearing."
    </i>
  </p>
  <p>Never finished, merely abandoned, indeed.</p>

  <h2>The muddy bit in the middle</h2>
  <p>
    There is, it should be said, a lacuna in the record during the seventies and
    eighties. There may well have been official glossaries maintained during
    that time, and if you know of one, and even better if you have one, I'd love
    to see it.
  </p>
  <p>
    At some point, probably in the nineties based on certain technological
    markers in the document, a new glossary was created. Once again, it was
    produced by a private concern; a well-known company that, for many years,
    was the primary contractor of field linguist services to the EOIR and, if I
    understand correctly, many branches of the US Government.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unfortunately, someone in the Legal department of this concern was awake and
    paying attention, and there is a clear note in the footer of every page of
    the copy of this glossary that I have:
    <i>"This glossary is the property of </i>
    <strong>redacted</strong>.
    <i>
      The copy or distribution of this document to other parties is strictly
      prohibited."
    </i>
  </p>
  <p>
    Hey -- I just received the document, no idea how. If I can determine that
    the document is, at this point, available elsewhere, I will link to it and
    discuss it more in depth. For the time being, however, I'll beg your
    forgiveness for not wanting to pick a fight with a company that has a much
    more well-funded legal department than I do.
  </p>

  <h2>The EOIR glossary, parts I and II</h2>
  <p>
    Now we come back to where I came into the story. By the time I arrived at
    the EOIR <s>en un momento del cual no quiero acordarme</s> towards the end
    of the second decade of the 21st century, the EOIR had produced their own
    glossary. I've provided it here in the form in which I received it, in two
    parts, as
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/EOIR Glossary pt I.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      "EOIR Glossary Part I"
    </a>
    and
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/EOIR Glossary pt II.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      "EOIR Glossary Part II"
    </a>
    .
  </p>
  <p>
    Arguably the most relevant glossary of those I've seen to the actual work of
    the linguists working in that context, it included all kinds of
    terminological gold: official renditions of US federal agencies' names,
    gnarly case law references, and even country-specific sections that gave an
    interpreter drowning in the details of the Colombian civil war a lifeline to
    an adequate rendition.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why is it in two parts? I'm not sure about that. It might have been all in
    one, but I scanned it wrong. Or, you know -- never finished, merely
    abandoned...
  </p>

  <h2>The 2020 EOIR Glossary</h2>
  <p>Back on track, picking up the story where I left it off:</p>
  <p>
    I started with what I had available: the EOIR's glossary, the
    contractor-that-shall-not-be-named glossary, and
    <a
      href="/resources/historical-glossaries/Loos Immigration Glossary  5-07.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      a glossary that I had been trained on
    </a>
    , briefly, a decade before by the very talented and capable Scott R. Loos.
  </p>
  <p>
    I made a spreadsheet, and entered every term from each glossary, aligning
    them where there were duplicates, eliminating terms that were so
    long-forgotten or obsolete that they should not even be there (fewer than
    you would think, actually), and most vitally perhaps, beginning to collect
    terms, conflicts, explanations, and differentiations from our day-to-day
    work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then I got the Berlitz glossaries. Then I started looking at source
    materials: Official UNHCR documents translated into multiple languages. My
    master spreadsheet of comparative glossaries went from a cool research
    assignment to the stuff of nightmares, red yarn and pushpins on a bulletin
    board.
  </p>
  <img
    src="/resources/historical-glossaries/thumbnails/stuff-of-nightmares.png"
    alt="A glimpse of madness: comparing seven glossaries and tracking acronyms"
    width="100%"
    height="auto"
  />
  <p>
    At the time, I did not have the technical skill to create the kind of
    metadata-enabled backend that the project needed, so I settled on an
    in-between solution of a spreadsheet with a "Master Terms List" tab, and
    then "breakout", topic-specific tabs for the domain-specific cul-de-sacs
    that kept us up at night: "Decisions and Appeals"; "Sex Crimes"; "Grounds
    and Standards of Proof"; "Nationality and Status"; "Comparative Hierarchical
    Denominations of Territorial Organization in Central America" (ok that last
    one I never got around to, but it's arguably the single most difficult one
    that comes up in almost every damn hearing).
  </p>
  <p>
    Those tabs are reflected in this version of the glossary through the
    <strong>Categories</strong> that you can see on the home page.
  </p>
  <p>
    So: For the Record, here is the original dedication I included in the
    version of the glossary that I, finally, shared to the rest of the EOIR,
    redacted only to protect the names of the innocent. Scott Loos, as well, is
    innocent, but is also a named author of one of these resources: I include
    his name in recognition of what he has done for our profession, and for me.
  </p>
  <p>
    <i>
      This glossary was composed by Staff Interpreters Oliver Renwick,
      <strong>redacted</strong>, and <strong>redacted</strong> of Cleveland
      EOIR. Of inestimable assistance were the consultative opinions, offered
      happily and frequently, of Immigration Judge
      <strong>redacted</strong> and Attorney Advisor
      <strong>redacted</strong>. Scott Loos provided my initial basis in
      Immigration terminology and invaluable training in terminology research,
      and <strong>redacted</strong> and
      <strong>redacted</strong> provided some excellent historical and
      explanatory terminology bases. Other EOIR interpreters made additional
      contributions, which I endeavoured to note in the text, and I am sure I am
      forgetting others who helped explain key concepts to me.
      <br />
      <br />
      The intention of this glossary is not prescriptive or restrictive; rather,
      it seeks to serve as a reference and a starting point for further
      development and elucidation of the twisted terminological webs of this
      field of law, administration, and procedure. More than anything else, as I
      drafted the explanations, I thought of the interpreter who has been newly
      hired to the EOIR, and can't make heads or tails of what the difference is
      between an admission and a concession, or why people aren't following the
      Federal Rules of Evidence.
      <br />
      <br />
      In composing this glossary, I began by compiling every term from every
      official and otherwise reputable Immigration glossary I could find--seven
      or eight in total--cross-referenced all of them for consensus on terms,
      and began researching, revising, and occasionally deleting.
      <br />
      <br />I hope this glossary is the next step in what is already a long
      history of terminology study and standardization in the immigration
      agencies of the United States. -Oliver JL Renwick
    </i>
  </p>
